{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-48020" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "On which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2)^{2}?$ \n\n- $[-1,0]$\n- $(-2,1]$\n- $[-3,2]$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13994892/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef6849b122e1deeb636b80da59f5dc1a4238f1ad3b9a6d2ddf8fbf5ebf694095"} {"question" "q-48020" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous. \n\nWith that in mind, let's take a look at each interval.\n\n- The interval $[-1,0]$ is closed, and the function is continuous on $[-1,0]$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable.\n\n- The interval $(-2,1]$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The interval $[-3,1]$ is closed, but the function is not continuous on it because $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2)^{2}$ is not defined at $x=-2.$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13994892/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef6849b122e1deeb636b80da59f5dc1a4238f1ad3b9a6d2ddf8fbf5ebf694095"} {"question" "q-48020" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13994892/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3a048e4bed580fc94c8cabfbd3cadcf794ad8cfeb1312b735a7531f161781089"} {"question" "q-48020" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13994892/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef6849b122e1deeb636b80da59f5dc1a4238f1ad3b9a6d2ddf8fbf5ebf694095"} {"question" "q-48020" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13994892/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef6849b122e1deeb636b80da59f5dc1a4238f1ad3b9a6d2ddf8fbf5ebf694095"} {"question" "q-48020" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13994892/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef6849b122e1deeb636b80da59f5dc1a4238f1ad3b9a6d2ddf8fbf5ebf694095"} {"question" "q-73531" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{2}^{x-1}-1}, & x\\ne 1 \\\\ \\,\\,1, & x=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n\n- $[-2,2]$\n- $[-3,1)$\n- $[1.5,2]$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13994892/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef6849b122e1deeb636b80da59f5dc1a4238f1ad3b9a6d2ddf8fbf5ebf694095"} {"question" "q-73531" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals. \n\n- Let first consider the interval  $[-2,2].$ This is a  closed interval, but we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}\\frac{1}{{2}^{x-1}-1}=\\infty \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand so $f(x)$ has an essential discontinuity at $x=1.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[-1,1]$, and the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The interval $[-3,1)$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable in this case.\n\n- Finally consider  $[1.5,2].$ The only discontinuity of the function $f(x)$ is at $x=1,$ which is not in the interval  $[1.5,2].$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is continuous on the interval  $[1.5,2]$, and the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"III only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13994892/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef6849b122e1deeb636b80da59f5dc1a4238f1ad3b9a6d2ddf8fbf5ebf694095"} {"question" "q-73531" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13994892/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3a048e4bed580fc94c8cabfbd3cadcf794ad8cfeb1312b735a7531f161781089"} {"question" "q-73531" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13994892/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef6849b122e1deeb636b80da59f5dc1a4238f1ad3b9a6d2ddf8fbf5ebf694095"} {"question" "q-73531" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13994892/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef6849b122e1deeb636b80da59f5dc1a4238f1ad3b9a6d2ddf8fbf5ebf694095"} {"question" "q-73531" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13994892/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef6849b122e1deeb636b80da59f5dc1a4238f1ad3b9a6d2ddf8fbf5ebf694095"} {"question" "q-129177" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "On which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function $y=\\frac{{x}^{4}}{x-3}?$ \n\n- $[1,5]$\n- $[-5,-1]$\n- $(-5,3)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13994892/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef6849b122e1deeb636b80da59f5dc1a4238f1ad3b9a6d2ddf8fbf5ebf694095"} {"question" "q-129177" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's take a look at each interval.\n\n- The interval $[1,5]$ is closed, but the function is not continuous on $[1,5]$ because this interval contains points where the function is not defined. In particular, it is not defined at $x=3.$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The interval $[-5,-1]$ is closed, and the function is continuous on $[-5,-1].$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable.\n\n- The interval $(-5,3)$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13994892/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef6849b122e1deeb636b80da59f5dc1a4238f1ad3b9a6d2ddf8fbf5ebf694095"} {"question" "q-129177" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13994892/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3a048e4bed580fc94c8cabfbd3cadcf794ad8cfeb1312b735a7531f161781089"} {"question" "q-129177" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13994892/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef6849b122e1deeb636b80da59f5dc1a4238f1ad3b9a6d2ddf8fbf5ebf694095"} {"question" "q-129177" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13994892/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef6849b122e1deeb636b80da59f5dc1a4238f1ad3b9a6d2ddf8fbf5ebf694095"} {"question" "q-129177" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13994892/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef6849b122e1deeb636b80da59f5dc1a4238f1ad3b9a6d2ddf8fbf5ebf694095"} {"question" "q-129218" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "On which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function $y=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2)}{x-2}?$ \n\n- $[-3,-1]$\n- $[-1,1]$\n- $[1,3]$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13994892/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef6849b122e1deeb636b80da59f5dc1a4238f1ad3b9a6d2ddf8fbf5ebf694095"} {"question" "q-129218" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's take a look at each interval.\n\n- The interval $[-3,-1]$ is closed, but the function is not defined on the part of the interval where $x\\le -2.$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The interval $[-1,1]$ is closed, and the function is continuous on $[-1,1].$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable.\n\n- The interval $[1,3]$ is closed, but the function is not defined at $x=2.$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13994892/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef6849b122e1deeb636b80da59f5dc1a4238f1ad3b9a6d2ddf8fbf5ebf694095"} {"question" "q-129218" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13994892/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3a048e4bed580fc94c8cabfbd3cadcf794ad8cfeb1312b735a7531f161781089"} {"question" "q-129218" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13994892/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef6849b122e1deeb636b80da59f5dc1a4238f1ad3b9a6d2ddf8fbf5ebf694095"} {"question" "q-129218" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13994892/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef6849b122e1deeb636b80da59f5dc1a4238f1ad3b9a6d2ddf8fbf5ebf694095"} {"question" "q-129218" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13994892/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef6849b122e1deeb636b80da59f5dc1a4238f1ad3b9a6d2ddf8fbf5ebf694095"} {"question" "q-131069" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{8}^{x}-61, & x<2 \\\\ {3}^{x-1}, & x\\ge 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n\n- $[3,5]$\n- $[1,3]$\n- $(-1,1)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13994892/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef6849b122e1deeb636b80da59f5dc1a4238f1ad3b9a6d2ddf8fbf5ebf694095"} {"question" "q-131069" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals.\n\n- Consider $[3,5].$ In this interval, $f(x)={3}^{x-1},$ so the function is continuous on the interval $[3,5].$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\n- Now, consider $[1,3].$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}({8}^{x}-61)={8}^{2}-61=3, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}{3}^{x-1}={3}^{2-1}=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits from the left and from the right of $x=2$ are the same. Hence, $f(x)$ is continuous on the interval $[1,3],$ and the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\n- Finally, the interval $(-1,1)$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13994892/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef6849b122e1deeb636b80da59f5dc1a4238f1ad3b9a6d2ddf8fbf5ebf694095"} {"question" "q-131069" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13994892/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3a048e4bed580fc94c8cabfbd3cadcf794ad8cfeb1312b735a7531f161781089"} {"question" "q-131069" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13994892/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef6849b122e1deeb636b80da59f5dc1a4238f1ad3b9a6d2ddf8fbf5ebf694095"} {"question" "q-131069" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13994892/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef6849b122e1deeb636b80da59f5dc1a4238f1ad3b9a6d2ddf8fbf5ebf694095"} {"question" "q-131069" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13994892/state.json" "file_sha256" "ef6849b122e1deeb636b80da59f5dc1a4238f1ad3b9a6d2ddf8fbf5ebf694095"}] "usage" {} "basis" 1066 "retractions" [] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
